notes="This landscape music-paper sheet records commutators and anticommutators of d, delta, lambda, multiplication operators mu and a degree operator, with hand-drawn verification dots/stars, trials and enclosures. The right column develops the q=1 divided-power construction and coefficient expansion; H stops after its first expansion line. The visible p exponents in the upper sequence and *+ in the short vertical map are retained without supplying -1 or a final 1. The last tensor factor is left as Phi_alpha/Psi_alpha because its loop and lower label do not warrant Psi_* with confidence. The lower degree calculation actually uses varphi and i, not H’s Theta and 1; this can be read as the general homogeneous-degree calculation. Directly, for an operator L of degree i, D(Lx)-L(Dx)=iLx on homogeneous x, so this general calculation is consistent. Separately, the divided-power multiplication rule phi phi^(q-1)=q phi^(q) gives the q_alpha factor in the lower underbrace, conditional on the indicated multiplication map. The finite sum of these coefficients is p when sum q_alpha=p. Neither calculation proves the unproved splitting arrows marked ? or a normal-form basis theorem. The original stars are author marks, not checks performed by this run. The cramped derivative underbrace, second numerical count, functional multiplication trial and cancelled indices remain doubtful. The crop named gamma landed on the adjacent argument; no second crop was made, and the small gamma subscript remains unresolved from the half. Manuscript foot 347; no date or page-count title."
commit(n,t,ds,cc,items,notes,hand=['Small low wedge beside barT is distinct from a multiplication dot.','Phi can have a strongly one-sided oval and resemble P; the lower vertical map gives a secure example, but the upper sequence remains marked Phi/Psi.','The degree calculation uses a long-stemmed varphi while the other relations use the oval barred Theta; the difference is retained.','Continuation of a product is often a single dash; the right-column products and q condition have these dashes.'],notation=[('barT_wedge','Low wedge denotes the displayed lambda operator.'),('varphi and Theta','Distinct forms retained in their separate calculation blocks.'),('Gamma^{*+}','Both vertical-map exponents visibly end at plus.'),('C_ij','Uppercase coefficient in mu_i mu_j relation.'),('F_2','Doubled F with lower 2, in the exponent-index margin.')],abbr=[('deg','degré'),('Supp','support')],checks=[check('346.degree-commutator','For homogeneous x of degree r and L of degree i: D(Lx)=(r+i)Lx and LD(x)=rLx, hence commutator iLx. Conditional check of the general calculation only.','targeted proof check'),check('346.pd-lowering','Applying phi_alpha phi_alpha^(q_alpha-1)=q_alpha phi_alpha^(q_alpha) termwise gives the displayed lower coefficient sum; sum q_alpha=p then gives p times the common monomial, conditional on that multiplication map.','targeted proof check')],unresolved=[r'Cancelled mu_i d and lambda trials in the fourth relation (low).',r'Small functional multiplication trial in the box (low).',r'Added cancelled q/p trial above the right product (low).',r'Added cancelled epsilon beside the upper Lambda exponent (low).',r'Last factor of the upper sequence: Phi_alpha or Psi_alpha (low).',r'Cancelled index lists, including the added second row (low).'])
